"The algebra of the monoid of order-preserving functions on an n-set and other reduced E-Fountain semigroups | Semigroup Forum | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00233-025-10595-2)\nciteturn10161view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn10160view0\",\"lineno\":403}); Total lines: 800\nL376: ### Lemma 4.2\nL377: \nL378: Let S be a reduced E-Fountain semigroup where the set E is central then S satisfies the generalized right ample condition.\nL379: ### Proof\nL380: \nL381: Assume \\((ba)^{*}=a^{*}\\) for \\(a,b\\in S\\). We need to prove \\((ba^{+})^{*}=a^{+}\\) which is equivalent to \\((ba^{*})^{*}=a^{*}\\). Since \\(ba^{*}a^{*}=ba^{*}\\) it is clear that \\((ba^{*})^{*}\\le a^{*}\\). On the other hand\nL382: \nL383: $$ ba(ba^{*})^{*}=ba^{*}a(ba^{*})^{*}=ba^{*}(ba^{*})^{*}a=ba^{*}a=ba $$\nL384: \nL385: so\nL386: \nL387: $$ a^{*}=(ba)^{*}\\le (ba^{*})^{*}. $$\nL388: \nL389: Therefore \\((ba^{*})^{*}=a^{*}\\) as required. \\(\\square \\)\nL390: Since the set E is commutative the relation \\(\\trianglelefteq _{l}\\) is a partial order. We have checked the requirements of Theorem cite60†3.12 so \\(\\Bbbk S\\simeq \\Bbbk _{0}\\mathcal {C}(S)\\) for every commutative unital ring \\(\\Bbbk \\). In this special case, \\(s^{+}=s^{*}\\) for every \\(s\\in S\\) so every non-zero morphism in \\(\\mathcal {C}(S)\\) is an endomorphism.\nL391: In particular, the set of identity morphisms \\(\\{C(e)\\mid e\\in E\\)} forms an orthogonal set of central idempotents of \\(\\Bbbk _{0}\\mathcal {C}(S)\\) so\nL392: $$ \\Bbbk _{0}\\mathcal {C}(S)\\simeq \\prod _{e\\in E}\\Bbbk _{0}\\mathcal {C}(S)C(e)\\simeq \\prod _{e\\in E}\\Bbbk _{0}\\mathcal {C}(S)(e,e) $$\nL393: \nL394: as \\(\\Bbbk \\)-algebras. We deduce that:\nL395: ### Theorem 4.3\nL396: \nL397: Let S be a finite semigroup with central idempotents such that \\(S^{2}=S\\) then\nL398: \nL399: $$ \\Bbbk S\\simeq \\prod _{e\\in E}\\Bbbk _{0}\\mathcal {C}(S)(e,e). $$\nL400: \nL401: Theorem cite64†4.3 is precisely [cite39†30 , Theorem 6.5].\nL402: \nL403: ## 5 Additional examples\nL404: ### 5.1 The monoid of order-preserving functions\nL405: Let \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {T}}\\,}}_{n}\\) be the monoid of all functions \\(f:[n]\\rightarrow [n]\\) (where \\([n]=\\{1,\\ldots ,n\\}\\)). A function \\(f\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {T}}\\,}}_{n}\\) is called order-preserving if \\(i\\le j\\implies f(i)\\le f(j)\\) for every \\(i,j\\in [n]\\). We denote by \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) the submonoid of \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {T}}\\,}}_{n}\\) of all order-preserving functions.\nL406: In this section we will see that \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) is a reduced E-Fountain semigroup that satisfies the generalized left ample condition so by the dual of Theorem cite60†3.12 there is an isomorphism \\(\\Bbbk {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\simeq \\Bbbk _{0}\\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n})\\) of \\(\\Bbbk \\)-algebras for any commutative unital ring \\(\\Bbbk \\). We will also describe more explicitly the composition in \\(\\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n})\\).\nL407: Elementary observations\nL408: \nL409: Recall that the kernel of of a function \\(f\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) — \\(\\ker (f)\\) — is the equivalence relation on its domain defined by \\((i_{1},i_{2})\\in \\ker (f)\\) if and only if \\(f(i_{1})=f(i_{2})\\). Since f is order-preserving, the kernel classes of f are intervals — if \\(i_{1}\\le i_{2}\\le i_{3}\\) and \\((i_{1},i_{3})\\in \\ker (f)\\) then \\((i_{1},i_{2})\\in \\ker f\\) also.\nL410: Let \\(K_{1},\\ldots ,K_{l}\\) be the kernel classes of f. Let \\(y_{i}=f(K_{i}\\)) and assume that the indices are arranged such that \\(y_{1}<y_{2}<\\cdots <y_{l}\\). Choose \\(x_{i}\\) to be the maximal element of \\(K_{i}\\) and note that it must be the case that \\(x_{l}=n\\). Now, set\nL411: \nL412: $$ X=\\{x_{1},\\ldots ,x_{l}\\},\\quad Y=\\{y_{1},\\ldots ,y_{l}\\} $$\nL413: so from every \\(f\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) we can extract two sets \\(X,Y\\subseteq [n]\\) such that \\(|X|=|Y|\\) and \\(n\\in X\\). In the other direction, from two such sets \\(X=\\{x_{1},\\ldots ,x_{l}\\}\\) where \\(x_{l}=n\\) and \\({Y=\\{y_{1},\\ldots ,y_{l}\\}}\\) we can retrieve an f by setting\nL414: \nL415: $$ f(x)={\\left\\{ \\begin{array}{ll} y_{1} & x\\le x_{1}\\\\ y_{i} & x_{i-1}<x\\le x_{i}\\quad (2\\le i\\le l). \\end{array}\\right. } $$\nL416: As a conclusion, we observe that there is a one-to-one correspondence between functions \\(f\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) and pairs of sets \\(X,Y\\subseteq [n]\\) such that \\(|X|=|Y|\\) and \\(n\\in X\\). From now on we denote by \\(f_{Y,X}\\) the function associated with \\(X,Y\\subseteq [n]\\) (in contrary to [cite42†27 , Section 4.2] where this function was denoted \\(f_{X,Y})\\).\nL417: Note that \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\textsf{im}}\\,}}(f_{Y,X})=Y\\) (where \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\textsf{im}}\\,}}(f)\\) is the image of f) and X contains the maximal elements of the kernel classes of \\(f_{Y,X}\\). Another easy observation is that \\(f_{Z,Y}f_{Y,X}=f_{Z,X}\\).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe algebra of the monoid of order-preserving functions on an n-set and other reduced E-Fountain semigroups | Semigroup Forum | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00233-025-10595-2)\nciteturn10161view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn10160view0\",\"lineno\":540}); Total lines: 800\nL525: Associated category\nL526: \nL527: We want to show now that we can give a simple description of the composition in \\(\\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n})\\). We start with some useful lemmas.\nL528: ### Lemma 5.9\nL529: \nL530: Let \\(f_{W,Z},f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\). If \\((f_{W,Z})^{*}=(f_{Y,X})^{+}\\) and \\(f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}=f_{W,X}\\).\nL531: ### Proof\nL532: \nL533: If \\((f_{W,Z})^{*}=(f_{Y,X})^{+}\\) then \\(Z=\\widetilde{Y}\\) (recall that \\(\\widetilde{Y}=Y\\cup \\{n\\}\\)) but \\(f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) implies \\(n\\in Y\\) so \\(\\widetilde{Y}=Y\\). Therefore \\(Z=Y\\) and \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}=f_{W,X}\\). \\(\\square \\)\nL534: \nL535: For every non-empty \\(W\\subseteq [n]\\) we set \\(\\max (W)\\) to be the maximal element of W and \\(W^{\\prime }=W\\backslash \\{\\max (W)\\}\\).\nL536: ### Lemma 5.10\nL537: \nL538: Let \\(f_{W,Z},f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\). If \\((f_{W,Z})^{*}=(f_{Y,X})^{+}\\) and \\(f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}=f_{W^{\\prime },X}\\).\nL539: ### Proof\nL540: \nL541: If \\((f_{W,Z})^{*}=(f_{Y,X})^{+}\\) then \\(Z=\\widetilde{Y}\\) but \\(f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) implies \\(n\\notin Y\\) so \\(f_{W,Z}(n)=\\max (W)\\) is not in the image of \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}\\). Every \\(w\\in W^{\\prime }\\) is the image of some \\(z\\in Z\\backslash \\{n\\}=Y\\) and therefore w is in the image of \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}\\). It is now clear that \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}=f_{W^{\\prime },X}\\). \\(\\square \\)\nL542: ### Lemma 5.11\nL543: \nL544: Let \\(f_{W,Z},f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) such that \\((f_{W,Z})^{*}=(f_{Y,X})^{+}\\). If \\(f_{W,Z}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) and \\(f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) or vice versa then \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\).\nL545: ### Proof\nL546: If \\(f_{W,Z}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) and \\(f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then \\(\\max (W)=n\\) and \\(W^{\\prime }=W\\backslash \\{n\\}\\). By Lemma cite79†5.10 we deduce \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}=f_{W\\backslash \\{n\\},X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\).\nL547: If \\(f_{W,Z}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then the image of \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}\\) which is contained in the image of \\(f_{W,Z}\\) does not contain n so \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}.\\) \\(\\square \\)\nL548: ### Lemma 5.12\nL549: \nL550: Let \\(f_{W,Z},f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\). If \\((f_{W,Z})^{*}=(f_{Y,X})^{+}\\) and at least one of \\(f_{W,Z},f_{Y,X}\\) is in \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then \\((f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X})^{+}=(f_{W,Z})^{+}\\). If \\(f_{W,Z},f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then \\((f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X})^{+}\\ne (f_{W,Z})^{+}\\).\nL551: ### Proof\nL552: If \\(f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then Lemma cite80†5.9 implies \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}=f_{W,X}\\) so \\((f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X})^{+}=e_{\\widetilde{W}}=(f_{W,Z})^{+}\\). If \\(f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then \\(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X}=f_{W^{\\prime },X}\\) by Lemma cite79†5.10 so \\((f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X})^{+}=e_{\\widetilde{W^{\\prime }}}\\) where \\((f_{W,Z})^{+}=e_{\\widetilde{W}}\\).\nL553: If \\(f_{W,Z}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then \\(\\max (W)=n\\) and \\(W^{\\prime }=W\\backslash \\{n\\}\\) so \\(\\widetilde{W^{\\prime }}=W=\\widetilde{W}\\) and we obtain \\((f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X})^{+}=(f_{W,Z})^{+}\\). If \\(f_{W,Z},f_{Y,X}\\notin {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal{O}\\mathcal{F}}\\,}}_{n}\\) then \\(\\max (W)\\ne n\\) so \\(\\max (W)\\notin \\widetilde{W^{\\prime }}\\) hence \\(\\widetilde{W^{\\prime }}\\ne \\widetilde{W}\\) and \\({(f_{W,Z}f_{Y,X})^{+}\\ne (f_{W,Z})^{+}}\\). \\(\\square \\)\nL554: The graph \\(\\mathcal {C}({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n})\\) associated with \\({{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) can be described as follows. The objects are in one-to-one correspondence with the set of idempotents \\(E=\\{e_{X}\\}\\) (where \\(X\\subseteq [n]\\) such that \\(n\\in X\\)). For every \\(f_{Y,X}\\in {{\\,\\mathrm{\\mathcal {O}}\\,}}_{n}\\) we associate a non-zero morphism \\(C(f_{Y,X})\\) from \\(e_{X}\\) to \\(e_{\\widetilde{Y}}\\).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEndomorphisms of Hecke–Kiselman Monoids Associated to Simple Oriented Graphs (https://arxiv.org/html/2604.15497v1)\nciteturn10161view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn10160view1\",\"lineno\":615}); Total lines: 941\nL612: {}{}}{}{}{{}{}}}}}{{}}{}{}{}{}{}{}{}{{}}\\lxSVG@stroke\\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 281.66 0 L 324.97 0}{fill:none} {{}{{}}{}{}{{}}{{{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{\\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \\lxSVG@transformcm{1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{1.0}{234.85428pt}{0.0pt}\\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform=matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 324.97 0)} \\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.32pt} \\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-dasharray=none,stroke-dashoffset=0.0pt} \\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-linecap=round} \\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-linejoin=round} \\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M -1.66 2.21 C -1.52 1.38 0 0.14 0.42 0 C 0 -0.14 -1.52 -1.38 -1.66 -2.21}{fill:none} \\lxSVG@closescope  \\lxSVG@closescope }}{{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}}\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nL613: \\lxSVG@closescope {{\nL614: {}{}{}{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}\\hss}\\lxSVG@discardpath\\lxSVG@closescope \\hss}}\\lxSVG@closescope\\endpgfpicture}}.$$  |\nL615: In (cite17†Ganyushkin and Mazorchuk, 2011 , Theorem 1(viii)), it is shown that, in this case, $\\mathrm{HK}_{\\Theta}$ is isomorphic to the Catalan monoid $\\mathcal{C}_{n+1}$. The Catalan monoid $\\mathcal{C}_{n+1}$ is the monoid of all order-preserving and order-decreasing total transformations of the set $[n+1]$. That is, the set of all $f:[n+1]\\to[n+1]$ such that:\nL616: \nL617:   1. (i)\nL618: \nL619: $f(i)\\leq f(j)$ for $i,j\\in[n+1]$ with $i\\leq j$ (order-preserving);\nL620: \nL621:   2. (ii)\nL622: \nL623: $f(i)\\leq i$ for $i\\in[n+1]$ (order-decreasing).\nL624: The operation in $\\mathcal{C}_{n+1}$ is the usual composition and the unit element is the identity on $[n+1]$. For $f,g\\in\\mathcal{C}_{n+1}$, we denote by $fg=f\\circ g$.\nL625: \nL626: Catalan monoids and their presentations were studied in cite57†Solomon (1996) . In (cite58†Stanley, 1999 , 6.25(g)), it was shown that $|\\mathcal{C}_{n+1}|=C_{n+1}$, where $C_{n}=\\frac{1}{n+1}\\binom{2n}{n}$ denotes the $n$-th Catalan number.\nL627: In the proof of (cite17†Ganyushkin and Mazorchuk, 2011 , Theorem 1(vii)), the authors use the fact that the transformations $T_{i}$ for $i=1,2,\\dots,n$, defined by\nL628: \nL629:  | $$T_{i}(j)=\\begin{cases}i,&j=i+1,\\\\\nL630: j,&\\text{otherwise},\\end{cases}$$  |\nL631: \nL632: generate $\\mathcal{C}_{n+1}$. The corresponding isomorphism of monoids from $\\mathrm{HK}_{\\Theta}$ to $\\mathcal{C}_{n+1}$ is then given by the unique extension of the mapping $x_{i}\\mapsto T_{i}$ and we denote it by $\\tau$.\nL633: ### Step 1: Determine $A_{\\Theta}$ and idempotents\nL634: \nL635: Since $\\Theta$ is acyclic, we have $A_{\\Theta}=\\mathbbm{2}^{[n]}$. For nonempty $X=\\{i_{1},i_{2},\\dots,i_{k}\\}\\subseteq[n]$ with $i_{1}>i_{2}>\\dots>i_{k}$, we have that $i_{j}\\to i_{j^{\\prime}}$ implies $j>j^{\\prime}$. By Lemma cite38†2.7 , we have\nL636: \nL637:  | $$e_{X}=x_{i_{k}}\\dots x_{i_{2}}x_{i_{1}}.$$  |  | (4)\nL638: Hence, in this case, idempotents are written as products in which the indices of the generators increase from left to right. Observe that in the Kiselman monoid, we have the converse case. We also denote $T_{X}=\\tau(e_{X})$. In particular, $T_{\\emptyset}$ is the identity on $[n+1]$.\nL639: Since $\\tau$ is an isomorphism of monoids, $e_{X}e_{Y}$ is an idempotent in $\\mathrm{HK}_{\\Theta}$ if and only if $T_{X}T_{Y}$ is an idempotent in $\\mathcal{C}_{n+1}$ and $e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{X\\cup Y}$ if and only if $T_{X}T_{Y}=T_{X\\cup Y}$. By (cite49†3 ), we then have\nL640: \nL641:  | $$p(X,Y)\\iff e_{X}e_{Y}\\text{ is an idempotent}\\iff T_{X}T_{Y}\\text{ is an idempotent}\\iff T_{X}T_{Y}=T_{X\\cup Y}.$$  |  | (5)\nL642: ### Step 2: Determine the predicate $p$\nL643: \nL644: For $X\\subseteq[n]$, define the following set\nL645: \nL646:  | $$F_{X}=[n+1]\\setminus\\{x+1\\mid x\\in X\\}.$$  |\nL647: \nL648: Note that $1\\in F_{X}$ for any $X\\subseteq[n]$. For $a\\in[n+1]$, we have\nL649: \nL650:  | $$a\\in F_{X}\\iff a-1\\notin X.$$  |  | (6)\nL651: \nL652: For any $X,Y\\subseteq[n]$, we also have\nL653: \nL654:  | $$F_{X\\cup Y}=F_{X}\\cap F_{Y}.$$  |  | (7)\nL655: \nL656: ###### Proposition 4.1.\nL657: \nL658: Let $X\\subseteq[n]$. Then we have\nL659: \nL660:  | $$T_{X}(t)=\\max(F_{X}\\cap[t]),$$  |\nL661: \nL662: for all $t\\in[n+1]$.\nL663: ###### Proof.\nL664: \nL665: We prove the formula by induction on $|X|$. If $X=\\emptyset$, then we have $F_{X}=[n+1]$. Hence $F_{X}\\cap[t]=[t]$ for all $t\\in[n+1]$. Since $T_{X}$ is the identity on $[n+1]$, we indeed have\nL666: \nL667:  | $$T_{X}(t)=t=\\max([t])=\\max(F_{X}\\cap[t]).$$  |\nL668: \nL669: Let $|X|=r$ with $0<r\\leq n$ and assume that the formula in the statement holds for all sets $Y\\subseteq[n]$ with $|Y|<r$. Write $X$ as\nL670: \nL671:  | $$X=\\{i_{1},i_{2},\\dots,i_{r-1},i_{r}\\}$$  |\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHomomorphisms of Hecke–Kiselman Monoids Associated to Simple Oriented Graphs (https://arxiv.org/html/2604.15497v3)\nciteturn10161view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn10160view2\",\"lineno\":220}); Total lines: 684\nL185: ###### Lemma 2.9.\nL186: \nL187: Let $X\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$. Then for any ordering of the set $X=\\{x_{i_{1}},x_{i_{2}},\\dots,x_{i_{k}}\\}$ such that $x_{i_{j}}\\overset{\\Theta}{\\to}x_{i_{j^{\\prime}}}$ implies $j<j^{\\prime}$ for all $j,j^{\\prime}\\in\\{1,2,\\dots,k\\}$, we have\nL188: \nL189:  | $$e_{X}=x_{i_{1}}x_{i_{2}}\\dots x_{i_{k}}.$$  |\nL190: \nL191: Moreover, we have $e_{X}z=ze_{X}=e_{X}$ for any $z\\in\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Theta}$ with $c(z)\\subseteq X$.\nL192: ###### Proof.\nL193: \nL194: Denote by\nL195: \nL196:  | $$y=x_{i_{1}}x_{i_{2}}\\dots x_{i_{k}}.$$  |\nL197: \nL198: If $x_{i_{j}}\\in X$, then\nL199: \nL200:  | $$x_{i_{j+1}}x_{i_{j+2}}\\dots x_{i_{k}}$$  |\nL201: \nL202: is obtained by multiplying letters that do not admit arrows to $x_{i_{j}}$. Lemma cite36†2.2 implies\nL203: \nL204:  | $$yx_{i_{j}}=x_{i_{1}}\\dots x_{i_{j-1}}(x_{i_{j}}x_{i_{j+1}}\\dots x_{i_{k}}x_{i_{j}})=x_{i_{1}}\\dots x_{i_{j-1}}(x_{i_{j}}x_{i_{j+1}}\\dots x_{i_{k}})=y.$$  |\nL205: \nL206: By induction, we get\nL207: \nL208:  | $$yx_{l_{1}}x_{l_{2}}\\dots x_{l_{m}}=y,$$  |\nL209: for any $x_{l_{1}},x_{l_{2}},\\dots,x_{l_{m}}\\in X$. This proves $yz=y$ for any $z\\in\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Theta}$ with $c(z)\\subseteq X$.\nL210: \nL211: For any $x_{i_{j}}\\in X$,\nL212: \nL213:  | $$x_{i_{1}}x_{i_{2}}\\dots x_{i_{j-1}}$$  |\nL214: \nL215: is obtained by multiplying letters that do not admit arrows from $x_{i_{j}}$. Lemma cite36†2.2 implies\nL216: \nL217:  | $$x_{i_{j}}y=(x_{i_{j}}x_{i_{1}}\\dots x_{i_{j-1}}x_{i_{j}})x_{i_{j+1}}\\dots x_{i_{k}}=(x_{i_{1}}\\dots x_{i_{j-1}}x_{i_{j}})x_{i_{j+1}}\\dots x_{i_{k}}=y.$$  |\nL218: \nL219: By induction, we again obtain\nL220:  | $$x_{l_{1}}x_{l_{2}}\\dots x_{l_{m}}y=y,$$  |\nL221: \nL222: for any $x_{l_{1}},x_{l_{2}},\\dots,x_{l_{m}}\\in X$, which proves $zy=y$ for any $z\\in\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Theta}$ with $c(z)\\subseteq X$. Since $c(y)=X$, we have\nL223: \nL224:  | $$y^{2}=yy=y.$$  |\nL225: \nL226: Hence $y$ is an idempotent in $\\mathbf{HK}_{\\Theta}$. Since $c(y)=X$, it follows that $y=e_{X}$. ∎\nL227: ###### Definition 2.10.\nL228: \nL229: A word $w\\in F(V_{\\Theta})$ is said to have no arrows from right to left if\nL230: \nL231:  | $$w=x_{i_{1}}x_{i_{2}}\\dots x_{i_{k}},$$  |\nL232: \nL233: where $x_{i_{1}},x_{i_{2}},\\dots,x_{i_{k}}\\in F(V_{\\Theta})$ are letters, and if there is an arrow from $x_{i_{j}}$ to $x_{i_{j^{\\prime}}}$ then $j<j^{\\prime}$ for all $j,j^{\\prime}\\in\\{1,2,\\dots,k\\}$.\nL234: Moreover, for $X\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$, $w$ is said to be $X$-topological if $w$ has no arrows from right to left, $c(w)=X$, and $\\ell(w)=|X|$. In other words, $w$ is $X$-topological if there are no arrows from right to left and each letter $x_{i}$ with $x_{i}\\in X$ occurs in $w$ exactly once.\nL235: ###### Remark 2.11.\nL236: \nL237: If $X\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$, then $\\Theta[X]$ is an acyclic oriented graph and hence there exists an $X$-topological word. Also observe that the empty word is $\\emptyset$-topological.\nL238: \nL239: ###### Lemma 2.12.\nL240: \nL241: For $X\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$, a word $w\\in F(V_{\\Theta})$ is $X$-topological if and only if $w$ is a normal form of $e_{X}$.\nL242: ###### Proof.\nL243: \nL244: If $w$ is $X$-topological, then $\\pi(w)=e_{X}$ by Lemma cite39†2.9 . Moreover, since no letter in $w$ occurs more than once, no further elementary cancellations may be performed on $w$, and hence $w$ is a normal form of $e_{X}$.\nL245: Now assume $w$ is a normal form of $e_{X}$. Since $X\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$, there exists an $X$-topological word $v$ by Remark cite40†2.11 . Hence, there are no arrows from right to left in $v$, $c(v)=X$, and $\\ell(v)=|X|$. By the previous paragraph, $v$ is a normal form of $e_{X}$. By Theorem cite41†2.4 (cite42†i ), we have $w\\sim v$. Since elementary commutations only interchange letters that are not connected, the relative order of any two letters that are connected by an arrow is preserved under $\\sim$.\nL246: Hence, since $v$ has no arrows from right to left, the same is true for $w$. Since $\\sim$ also preserves content and length, we have $c(w)=c(v)=X$ and $\\ell(w)=\\ell(v)=|X|$. This implies that $w$ is $X$-topological. ∎\nL247: ###### Lemma 2.13.\nL248: \nL249: Let $X,Y\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$. If there are no arrows starting in $Y$ and ending in $X$ and $X\\cap Y=\\emptyset$, then $X\\cup Y\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$ and\nL250: \nL251:  | $$e_{X}e_{Y}=e_{X\\cup Y}.$$  |\nL252: ###### Proof.\nL253: Since $X\\cap Y=\\emptyset$ and there are no arrows starting in $Y$ and ending in $X$, any oriented cycle in $\\Theta[X\\cup Y]$ must entirely lie in either $X$ or $Y$. Since $X,Y\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$, it follows that there are no oriented cycles in $\\Theta[X\\cup Y]$, and hence $X\\cup Y\\in\\mathcal{A}_{\\Theta}$. Let $w_{X}$ be a normal form of $e_{X}$ and let $w_{Y}$ be a normal form of $e_{Y}$. Lemma cite43†2.12 implies that $w_{X}$ is $X$-topological and $w_{Y}$ is $Y$-topological.\n"
